NNAAMMEE
     ttbbll — tbl language reference for mandoc

DDEESSCCRRIIPPTTIIOONN
     The ttbbll language formats tables.  It is used within mdoc(7) and man(7)
     pages.  This manual describes the subset of the ttbbll language accepted by
     the mandoc(1) utility.

     Each table is started with a roff(7) TTSS macro, consist of at most one
     line of _O_p_t_i_o_n_s, one or more _L_a_y_o_u_t lines, one or more _D_a_t_a lines, and
     ends with a TTEE macro.  All input must be 7-bit ASCII.

   OOppttiioonnss
     If the first input line of a table ends with a semicolon, it contains
     case-insensitive options separated by spaces, tabs, or commas.  Other‐
     wise, it is interpreted as the first _L_a_y_o_u_t line.

     The following options are available.  Some of them require arguments en‐
     closed in parentheses:

     aallllbbooxx  Draw a single-line box around each table cell.

     bbooxx     Draw a single-line box around the table.  For GNU compatibility,
             this may also be invoked with ffrraammee.

     cceenntteerr  Center the table instead of left-adjusting it.  For GNU compati‐
             bility, this may also be invoked with cceennttrree.

     ddeecciimmaallppooiinntt
             Use the single-character argument as the decimal point with the nn
             layout key.  This is a GNU extension.

     ddeelliimm   Use the two characters of the argument as eqn(7) delimiters.
             Currently unsupported.

     ddoouubblleebbooxx
             Draw a double-line box around the table.  For GNU compatibility,
             this may also be invoked with ddoouubblleeffrraammee.

     eexxppaanndd  Increase the width of the table to the current line length.  Cur‐
             rently ignored.

     lliinneessiizzee
             Draw lines with the point size given by the unsigned integer ar‐
             gument.  Currently ignored.

     nnookkeeeepp  Allow page breaks within the table.  This is a GNU extension and
             currently ignored.

     nnoossppaacceess
             Ignore leading and trailing spaces in data cells.  This is a GNU
             extension.

     nnoowwaarrnn  Suppress warnings about tables exceeding the current line length.
             This is a GNU extension and currently ignored.

     ttaabb     Use the single-character argument as a delimiter between data
             cells.  By default, the horizontal tabulator character is used.

   LLaayyoouutt
     The table layout follows an _O_p_t_i_o_n_s line or a roff(7) TTSS or TT&& macro.
     Each layout line specifies how one line of _D_a_t_a is formatted.  The last
     layout line ends with a full stop.  It also applies to all remaining data
     lines.  Multiple layout lines can be joined by commas on a single physi‐
     cal input line.

     Each layout line consists of one or more layout cell specifications, op‐
     tionally separated by whitespace.  The following case-insensitive key
     characters start a new cell specification:

     cc   Center the string in this cell.

     rr   Right-justify the string in this cell.

     ll   Left-justify the string in this cell.

     nn   Justify a number around its last decimal point.  If no decimal point
         is found in the number, it is assumed to trail the number.

     ss   Horizontally span columns from the last non-ss layout cell.  It is an
         error if a column span follows a __ or == cell, or comes first on a
         layout line.  The combined cell as a whole consumes only one cell of
         the corresponding data line.

     aa   Left-justify a string and pad with one space.

     ^^   Vertically span rows from the last non-^^ layout cell.  It is an error
         to invoke a vertical span on the first layout line.  Unlike a hori‐
         zontal span, a vertical span consumes a data cell and discards the
         content.

     __   Draw a single horizontal line in this cell.  This consumes a data
         cell and discards the content.  It may also be invoked with --.

     ==   Draw a double horizontal line in this cell.  This consumes a data
         cell and discards the content.

     Each cell key may be followed by zero or more of the following case-in‐
     sensitive modifiers:

     bb   Use a bold font for the contents of this cell.

     dd   Move content down to the last row of this vertical span.  Currently
         ignored.

     ee   Make this column wider to match the maximum width of any other column
         also having the ee modifier.

     ff   The next one or two characters select the font to use for this cell.
         One-character font names must be followed by a blank or period.  See
         the roff(7) manual for supported font names.

     ii   Use an italic font for the contents of this cell.

     mm   Specify a cell start macro.  This is a GNU extension and currently
         unsupported.

     pp   Set the point size to the following unsigned argument, or change it
         by the following signed argument.  Currently ignored.

     vv   Set the vertical line spacing to the following unsigned argument, or
         change it by the following signed argument.  Currently ignored.

     tt   Do not vertically center content in this vertical span, leave it in
         the top row.  Currently ignored.

     uu   Move cell content up by half a table row.  Currently ignored.

     ww   Specify a minimum column width.

     xx   After determining the width of all other columns, distribute the rest
         of the line length among all columns having the xx modifier.

     zz   Do not use this cell for determining the width of this column.

     ||   Draw a single vertical line to the right of this cell.

     ||||  Draw a double vertical line to the right of this cell.

     If a modifier consists of decimal digits, it specifies a minimum spacing
     in units of nn between this column and the next column to the right.  The
     default is 3.  If there is a vertical line, it is drawn inside the spac‐
     ing.

   DDaattaa
     The data section follows the last _L_a_y_o_u_t line.  Each data line consists
     of one or more data cells, delimited by ttaabb characters.

     If a data cell contains only the two bytes ‘\^’, the cell above spans to
     this row, as if the layout specification of this cell were ^^.

     If a data cell contains only the single character ‘_’ or ‘=’, a single or
     double horizontal line is drawn across the cell, joining its neighbours.
     If a data cell contains only the two character sequence ‘\_’ or ‘\=’, a
     single or double horizontal line is drawn inside the cell, not joining
     its neighbours.  If a data line contains nothing but the single character
     ‘_’ or ‘=’, a horizontal line across the whole table is inserted without
     consuming a layout row.

     In place of any data cell, a text block can be used.  It starts with TT{{
     at the end of a physical input line.  Input line breaks inside the text
     block neither end the text block nor its data cell.  It only ends if TT}}
     occurs at the beginning of a physical input line and is followed by an
     end-of-cell indicator.  If the TT}} is followed by the end of the physical
     input line, the text block, the data cell, and the data line ends at this
     point.  If the TT}} is followed by the ttaabb character, only the text block
     and the data cell end, but the data line continues with the data cell
     following the ttaabb character.  If TT}} is followed by any other character,
     it does not end the text block, which instead continues to the following
     physical input line.

EEXXAAMMPPLLEESS
     String justification and font selection:

           .TS
           rb c  lb
           r  ci l.
           r       center  l
           ri      ce      le
           right   c       left
           .TE

           rb   c    lb   r    ci   l.   r       center  l  ri      ce      le
           right   c       left

     Some ports in OpenBSD 6.1 to show number alignment and line drawing:

           .TS
           box tab(:);
           r| l
           r  n.
           software:version
           _
           AFL:2.39b
           Mutt:1.8.0
           Ruby:1.8.7.374
           TeX Live:2015
           .TE

           box tab(:); r| l r  n.   software:version  _  AFL:2.39b  Mutt:1.8.0
           Ruby:1.8.7.374 TeX Live:2015


     Spans and skipping width calculations:

           .TS
           box tab(:);
           lz  s | rt
           lt| cb| ^
           ^ | rz  s.
           left:r
           l:center:
           :right
           .TE

           box  tab(:);  lz   s  |  rt  lt| cb| ^ ^ | rz  s.  left:r l:center:
           :right


     Text blocks, specifying spacings and specifying and equalizing column
     widths, putting lines into individual cells, and overriding aallllbbooxx:

           .TS
           allbox tab(:);
           le le||7 lw10.
           The fourth line:_:line 1
           of this column:=:line 2
           determines:_:line 3
           the column width.:T{
           This text is too wide to fit into a column of width 17.
           T}:line 4
           T{
           No break here.
           T}::line 5
           .TE

           allbox  tab(:);  le  le||7  lw10.  The fourth line:_:line 1 of this
           column:=:line 2 determines:_:line 3 the column width.:T{ This  text
           is  too  wide  to  fit  into a column of width 17.  T}:line 4 T{ No
           break here.  T}::line 5


     These examples were constructed to demonstrate many ttbbll features in a
     compact way.  In real manual pages, keep tables as simple as possible.
     They usually look better, are less fragile, and are more portable.

CCOOMMPPAATTIIBBIILLIITTYY
     The mandoc(1) implementation of ttbbll doesn't support mdoc(7) and man(7)
     macros and eqn(7) equations inside tables.

